# Governance Metadata Conventions

> **FAIR:** [`fairness_metadata.mdc`](fairness_metadata.mdc) — F2-01/F2-02 via `make validate-fair-metadata`; schema via `make validate-metadata-files-content`; metadata ↔ SQL via `make validate-lineage-consistency`; lake I1-01 after deploy.

## The Golden Rule
Every `.sql` file in `queries/{layer}/` **must** have a matching `.yml` in `metadata/{layer}/` with the same base name. CI fails without it.

## Common Required Fields (all layers)

```yaml
database_name: str          # e.g. "enrich_ebdb_proposal", "dw_rent", "metric_rent"
table_name: str             # table name without prefix
description: str            # min 10 characters
domain: str                 # must match valid domain (see below)
owner: str                  # email: user@quintoandar.com.br
columns:
  column_name:
    description: str        # min 10 characters
    privacy:                # optional; required only when the column is PII
      piiType: str          # slug from governance/pii_catalog/pii_catalog.yml
      dataSubjectType:      # who the data is about (drives masking)
        - customer          # → must be masked in authX unless RAE in controls
        - employee          # classify PII, no Trino mask
        - partner           # classify PII, no Trino mask
```

> Optional `privacy` section — see [PII privacy section](#pii-privacy-section-preferred) below.

**Valid domains:** must **full-match** one allowlist value. The authoritative list lives in `packages/bietlejuice-core/src/bietlejuice/governance/domains.yml` (read via `bietlejuice.governance.domain_registry`) — do not hardcode it here. `METADATA_DOMAIN_CI_ALLOWLIST_PATTERN` in `fairness_assessment/constants.py` re-exports from that loader, and CI Yamale `*_schema.yml` is generated from it. Examples: `For Rent`, `Data Ops & Governance`, `Tech Platform`.

**Deprecated labels (do not use in new metadata):** `Governance` → `Data Ops & Governance`; `Platform` → `Data Platform` or `Tech Platform`; `Data Science` → `DS Pricing` or `MLOps`; `Support and Service` → `Support and Services`.

## PII privacy section (Phase 2 — infra only, do not suggest in normal PRs)

> **Rollout gate:** The catalog, Yamale schema, lineage checks, and `validate-pii-privacy` CI exist as **foundation**. **Metadata PII classification is not part of normal table authoring or PR review yet**. Do **not** add or suggest a `privacy` section on routine PRs. Only add `privacy` when the user or ticket **explicitly** requests PII classification (dedicated governance PR). CI validates `privacy` **only when it is already in the diff** (positive validation). Table-level `privacy.dataSubjectType` is **optional** until post-backfill gate.

When classification **is** explicitly in scope:

- **Table `privacy.dataSubjectType`** (optional) — YAML block list: `customer`, `employee`, `partner`. Preferred titular when backfilling.
- **Column `privacy.piiType`** — slug from the catalog; column `dataSubjectType` optional when table titular is set.
- **Lineage-first** — classify upstream first; downstream inherits `piiType` via `lineage`.

```yaml
columns:
  cpf:
    description: "Brazilian individual taxpayer identification number (CPF)."
    lineage:
      - datalake_rental_guarantee_platform_clean.propose_person.cpf
    privacy:
      piiType: cpf  # slug from pii_catalog.yml
      dataSubjectType: [customer]
```

Production masking is configured in **authX** (`commons/data/trino/prod.yaml`) after metadata merges to `master`. See [`governance/pii/README.md`](../../governance/pii/README.md).

### JSON columns (`jsonPaths`)

Use when the lake still stores a **JSON blob** and PII lives at specific paths. If SQL already explodes fields into scalar columns, use normal `privacy` on each column instead.

```yaml
columns:
  person_payload:
    description: "JSON document from the source API."
    lineage:
      - datalake_example_clean.person.person_payload
    privacy:
      jsonPaths:
        - path: $.holder.cpf
          piiType: cpf
          dataSubjectType: [customer]
        - path: $.holder.email
          piiType: personal_email
          dataSubjectType: [customer]
```

`id_entity` for a path: `datalake_db.table.column#$.holder.cpf`.

## Personal Data Handling

Do **not** add a `personal_data_classification` key to columns — the metadata validator rejects it and CI/lint fails. Do **not** encode a column's PII tier in its `description`. When a dedicated classification PR adds `privacy`, use `privacy.piiType`; CI derives LGPD tier from the catalog. **Do not** proactively add `privacy` on normal feature PRs.

### Personal Data Catalog (reference)

These tiers come from the QuintoAndar Personal Data Catalog ([`governance/pii_catalog/pii_catalog.yml`](../../governance/pii_catalog/pii_catalog.yml)). Each `piiType` slug maps to `classification`:

- **`sensitive`** — special-category data under LGPD Art. 11: racial/ethnic origin, political opinion, religion, gender identity, sexual orientation, union membership, health information, biometric data. Must never appear in metric/qube outputs or publicly-accessible schemas without documented LGPD approval.
- **`highly_personal`** — financially or legally sensitive data: bank statement, credit history, credit score, IRPF, INSS statement, criminal background, card numbers, photo of person with ID document. Prefer hashing or masking before promoting to enrich/dw.
- **`personal`** — standard personal data under LGPD Art. 5: name, CPF, date of birth, email, phone, address, RG, passport, geolocation, salary, device ID, IP, cookies, etc.

When `privacy` is not declared yet, infer sensitivity from column semantics, domain rules, or governance review — do **not** invent metadata classification to satisfy a control.

### LGPD controls — enforced today

| Control | When it applies | Where it lives |
|---|---|---|
| No raw PII in enrich/DW | New tables; use `sk_person` / join `dim_person` | SQL (`sql_conventions.mdc` §13) |
| `table_privileges` | Table carries `sensitive` or `highly_personal` data | DAG declaration |
| `privacy.k_anonymity ≥ 5` | Metric/qube output derived from `sensitive` data | Qube/metric declaration |
| `has_right_to_be_forgotten` guard | Reverse DAG exporting contact PII | SQL (`sql_conventions.mdc` §13); **exempt:** `dags/people/reverse_reports/` (`people_domain.mdc`) |

When a column has `privacy.piiType`, use the catalog-derived `classification` for `table_privileges` and `k_anonymity` decisions. The same logical column should use the same `piiType` and `dataSubjectType` across layers (raw → clean → enrich → dw).

### Examples

**Declaration** — `table_privileges` lives in the declaration, not in metadata:

```yaml
# fintech/cyber_*_declaration.yml
tables_customization:
  contracts:
    table_privileges:
      data-platform-engineers: ["SELECT"]
```

## Enrich & DW Columns — `lineage` required

`lineage` is required for every column whose value **derives from a source column**. It is **not** required for columns whose value is a hardcoded literal in the SQL (e.g. `'RENT' AS business_context`, `'FS_DILIGENCE' AS entity`, `'OWNER' AS persona`). Hardcoded values have no source column to trace.

```yaml
columns:
  id_contract:
    lineage:
      - datalake_ebdb_clean.contract.id    # format: database.table.column
    description: "Unique identifier for the contract."
  amount:
    lineage:
      - datalake_ebdb_clean.contract.rent_amount
    description: "Monthly rent amount in BRL."
    categories:                            # optional: for categorical columns
      ACTIVE: Currently active contract.
      CANCELED: Contract was canceled.
  business_context:
    # No lineage — hardcoded literal value ('RENT') in the SQL.
    description: "Business context. Always RENT for this entity."
```

## Metric Columns — `dimension` or `metric` block required
Each metric column must be **either** a dimension or a metric, never plain:

```yaml
# Dimension column (used to slice/group the metric)
week:
  lineage:
    - dw_public.dim_date.week_start
  description: "Week start date."
  dimension: true

# Metric column (the measurable value)
ongoing_contracts:
  description: "Count of unique contracts active during the week."
  metric:
    name: Ongoing Contracts Weekly
    description: "Count of unique contracts that were active each week."
    acronym: OC
    is_additive: false
    business_stage: Demand          # e.g. Supply, Demand, Transaction, Retention
    hierarchy: User analysis
    approved_by: owner@quintoandar.com.br
    link_to_metric: "https://quintoandar.sa.looker.com/looks/1234"  # optional
```

## Raw vs clean (Woodpecker / Yamale — not the same)

| | **raw** (`raw_schema.yml`) | **clean+** (`clean_schema.yml`, core, enrich, dw, metric) |
|--|---------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| Table `description` | Optional (Yamale) | Required, min 10 chars (Yamale). **FAIR F2-01:** substantive business context (entity/process, grain, main consumers) — same TDQ gate as columns at PR time |
| `owner` | Optional | Optional in schema; required for FAIR F2-01 when file is in PR diff |
| `columns` | Optional | Required |
| Column `description` | Optional (Yamale) | Required, min 10 chars (Yamale). **FAIR F2-02 (blocking on clean+):** substantive — business meaning, units/format, derivation logic; not name echo, status-only labels, or platform filler |
| `lineage` | Not in schema | Required on enrich/dw for derived columns |
| `tags` on columns | Allowed (e.g. Confidential) | — |

**Description quality (all layers where you document):** Yamale enforces **presence and min length** on clean+; FAIR enforces **substance** on table (F2-01) and columns (F2-02). Table and column text should explain *what* the data represents in business terms, *how* it is produced or derived, and *who* uses it — aligned to [`fairness_metadata.mdc`](fairness_metadata.mdc) and [`fair_requirement_glossary.md`](../../docs/governance/fair_requirement_glossary.md). Min 10 characters alone is not enough for clean+ in PR CI.

Many **raw** tables are ingested via **Spark jobs** without `queries/raw/*.sql` — lineage-consistency does not apply there. Prefer rich documentation on **clean / enrich / dw**; touch **raw** only when that YAML is in the PR diff or CI failed on it. See `fair-metadata/reference/woodpecker_layer_gates.md` and `fairness_metadata.mdc`.

## Validation Commands
```bash
# Check that every SQL file has a matching metadata file
make validate-metadata-files-exist

# Check that metadata content is valid against schema
make validate-metadata-files-content

# Check PII privacy sections (piiType, dataSubjectType, RAE controls)
make validate-pii-privacy

# Check SQL lineage consistency with metadata
make validate-lineage-consistency
```

## Common Mistakes
- `description` shorter than 10 characters → CI fails on **clean+**, not on raw (raw table description is optional in Yamale).
- Bulk-editing `metadata/raw/` with owner/domain/column text copied from clean when raw is not in the PR diff → unnecessary; Woodpecker would not require it.
- `owner` not matching email regex → CI fails.
- Missing `lineage` on an enrich/dw column that derives from a source → CI fails.
- Adding `lineage` to a hardcoded literal column (e.g. `'RENT' AS business_context`) → incorrect; remove it.
- Metric column without `dimension: true` or `metric:` block → CI fails.
- Metric SQL referencing raw/clean tables directly → lineage validation fails.
- Column containing personal data with no `privacy` section → **expected in Phase 1**; not a CI failure; **do not** suggest adding `privacy` in review or authoring.
- `personal_data_classification` on a column → remove (CI rejects the key). **Do not** suggest replacing it with `privacy` unless the PR is an explicit classification effort.
- When `privacy` **is** in the diff: `privacy.piiType` must be in `governance/pii_catalog/pii_catalog.yml` → else CI fails.
- `customer` in `dataSubjectType` without RAE → expect authX masking PR after merge to `master` (not in this repo).
- Storing raw PII (name, CPF, email, address, etc.) as a column in a new enrich/dw table instead of joining `dim_person` → governance review will block it.
- `sensitive` or `highly_personal` data in enrich/dw without `table_privileges` in the declaration → LGPD violation risk (use catalog-derived tier when `privacy` is declared, else infer from column semantics or domain rules).
- `sensitive` data exposed in a metric/qube output without `k_anonymity ≥ 5` → LGPD violation risk.
